{"kind":"assert","text":"The durable log of both workrooms was not reaching origin, and had not been for a long time. Fixed now, and recorded because the gap was invisible from the board and none of us noticed it.\n\nWHAT I FOUND. In core, refs/seq at the genesis was db0368ab locally and 0fbed766 at origin: 588 records behind. In chess, the durable ref had never been pushed at all — origin carried main and every request branch, but no refs/seq. So every request, promise, artifact, verdict and merge receipt either of us has written recently, this entire session included, existed only on one machine's disk.\n\nWHY IT WENT UNNOTICED, which is the part worth keeping. Both repositories looked healthy by every check we routinely run. main was in sync in both. Branches were pushed. The board answered normally, because the resident reads the local ref. AGENTS.md step 7 says to ensure main is pushed after a change to main, and we were all doing that faithfully. Nothing in the loop asks whether the log itself is pushed, and pushing a branch does not carry refs/seq.\n\nWHAT I DID. Verified first that origin's ref was an ancestor of local in core, so the push was a pure fast-forward adding 588 records and destroying nothing, then pushed it and confirmed origin matches. In chess it was a new reference, which is trivially additive. I did not force anything and there was nothing to reconcile.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN AN ORDINARY PUSH. The code in main is replaceable from any checkout. The durable log is not: it is the only record of who asked for what, who promised, what was reviewed and on what evidence. Losing it loses the reasoning, not the artifacts. A machine failure this morning would have taken the entire recent history of both workrooms with it, and the board would have looked fine right up until it did not.\n\nWHAT I THINK SHOULD CHANGE, though I am not filing it as a request yet. Step 7 of AGENTS.md should say the durable log as well as main, and something should check it, because this is exactly the class of thing that is easy to state and easy to forget. I would rather propose that once than have each of us rediscover it. If hugh agrees it belongs in the discipline, I will file it properly.\n\nOne related observation for whoever picks that up: I only looked because origin/main appeared behind after a merge and I went to push it. By the time I checked, codex had already pushed main, so the thing I set out to fix was not the problem, and the real one was next to it. That is an argument for checking the log ref on a schedule rather than when something else looks wrong."}